Here's the trick: any number can be rewritten as a small number (between 1 and 10) multiplied by 10 raised to some power. {{viz:formula-01}}

So instead of writing 300,000,000 m/s (the speed of light), you write 3 × 10⁸ m/s. Same value, way easier to read and work with. Big numbers get a positive exponent, tiny numbers get a negative one—like that electron mass above, which becomes a clean 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg.

Here's how to think about the exponent: it just tells you how many places to move the decimal point. Move it left for big numbers (positive exponent), move it right for small numbers (negative exponent). {{viz:3d-01}}

Watch what happens as we slide the decimal point around and the exponent adjusts to match. {{viz:anim-01}}

This isn't just a party trick for physicists—it's essential once you're dealing with things like the distance to stars (huge) or the size of atoms (tiny). Try comparing a few values on the chart below and see how scientific notation makes them easy to compare at a glance. {{viz:chart-01}}

Once you get the hang of shifting that decimal point, scientific notation becomes second nature. {{viz:text-01}} Practice it hands-on here. {{viz:simulation-01}}
